// C++17
#include <iostream>
#include <type_traits>

int main() {
    int x = 5;
    decltype(x) a = 1;       // declared type: int
    decltype((x)) b = x;     // (x) is an lvalue expression -> int&
    b = 9;                   // writes through to x
    std::cout << x << " " << std::is_same_v<decltype(a), int>
              << " " << std::is_same_v<decltype(b), int&>;
}

// C++17
#include <iostream>

int& liveRef(int& r) { return r; }

int main() {
    int px = 3;
    auto a = liveRef(px);    // auto strips the reference: a is a copy
    a = 10;                  // px untouched
    auto& b = liveRef(px);   // auto& keeps it: b aliases px
    b = 20;
    std::cout << px << " " << a << "\n";   // 20 10
}

independentExercise.solution:
(a) `a` is an **lvalue**; the temporary `Position{{10.5, 11.0}, "AAPL"}` is a **prvalue** (and, C++17, it initialises its destination directly with guaranteed elision); `std::move(a)` is an **xvalue** - a cast, nothing moved yet.

(b) The standard guarantees `a` is in a **valid but unspecified** state: it may be assigned to or destroyed safely. You may assume **nothing** about `a.fills`' size - implementations usually leave it empty, but that is not contractual.

(c)

```cpp
// C++17
for (auto&& f : b.fills) {
    // process f - no copies: auto&& binds the element itself
}
```

`auto&&` is the universal reference: whatever the range yields (here `double&`), it binds without copying.

